skills.purpose:
The executor-facing skill. It turns the objective contract's five-item design block and the cheap-falsifier-first lessons into three mechanical stage transitions: lock, roster and official. Body, about 180 lines:
(1) five non-negotiables;
(2) the computed tier table;
(3) the stage map from the user's loop to commands: explore (read KB/DELIVERY_LEDGER, FINDINGS and INCIDENTS, then fill e3_cadence and predictions) -> design (ENDPOINT_LOCK.json, gate --stage lock) -> cycle 0 (testbed screen before learner code) -> test cycles (free-form; technical receipts and ADMISSION.json, then dev OUTPUTS.jsonl and score --role dev; a cycle-3 rehearsal runs gate --stage official --dry) -> official (gate --stage official);
(4) a FAIL table mapping each check family to its allowed responses;
(5) forbidden moves;
(6) a 'not required' list against ceremony: no per-cycle audit files, no compliance disclaimers, no hand-written STATUS booleans.
Field-level schemas live once, in tools/minifly_check/SCHEMAS.md.

key_rules:
Admission before spend: no roster, pilot or official run beyond the tier-1 technical allowance until gate --stage roster passes. It reads raw receipts from one technical record per arm plus ADMISSION.json produced by the locked admit.py, and requires all of the following:
- every lesion arm makes zero writes on its declared channels, W makes more than zero, and the state digests differ;
- realized dose meets the declared minimum;
- each port's teaching moves the target margin relative to a passive event, for both signs;
- a zero coefficient gives a state bit-identical to the passive event;
- fixture parameters equal production_params;
- every gate has a demonstrated FAIL and a demonstrated PASS;
- input contrast and leave-one-out prompt ID are above chance by the locked margins;
- short-prefix taught acquisition beats the best shortcut;
- SHUF agrees with the truth at about chance.

An outcome-informed change is an amendment. Any change to primary task, metric, pool, checkpoint, comparators, margin, floor, rule or guards after an outcome artifact exists needs an AMENDMENTS.jsonl row with these properties:
- it lists every result seen;
- it keeps the old endpoint as co-primary;
- it bumps protocol_id;
- it cites a USER_DECISIONS id.
Any change that increases objective_distance on any axis (task family, output, held type, native path, feedback, OLD protection) needs a USER_DECISIONS id even before outcomes. Official worlds must be untouched by every amended protocol.

skills.purpose:
This skill stops claim inflation and stale summary layers, which are what agents read first. It does this by making the claim a generated, closed-enum object rather than prose. Body, about 120 lines:
(1) generate CLAIM.json and the headline with minifly_check.py claim <dir>;
(2) the vocabulary table and the level-earning rules from the objective contract;
(3) the banned-wording list;
(4) appending findings, narrowing them and propagating the narrowing;
(5) scoring predictions;
(6) the pointer v1 rewrite procedure with snapshot;
(7) the minimal verification subset to publish.
Prose reports stay free-form below the generated headline.

key_rules:
Closed vocabulary, with free-text evidence levels rejected:
- level_earned: E0, E1, E2, E3, E4 or NONE;
- primary_gate: PASS, FAIL, INVALID, INCOMPLETE, NOT_OPENED or CANNOT_TEST_CLAIM_AS_SPECIFIED;
- gate_type: objective or component;
- worlds_role: DEV, PILOT or SEALED_CONFIRM.

E2 or higher requires primary_gate PASS with gate_type objective. E3 additionally requires three things:
- the no-prior-teaching causal control passed;
- held items, after canonicalization, are absent from every feedback-bearing stream;
- headroom over the best shortcut is at least the margin.
Otherwise report the level actually earned.

skills.purpose:
This skill tests the testers, for the reviewer, the incident finder and anyone changing the kit. It keeps the strongest safeguard observed in the history: executing cross-agent review caught a substantive defect in every reviewed round (UF-28). It adds what that review lacked: identity enforcement, seeded canaries, reconciliation, and a closed path from incident to check. Body, about 150 lines:
(A) the tier-2 review protocol, timed after ADMISSION and before any spend, so that it covers both design and admission in one event;
(B) the canary run;
(C) REVIEW.json and reconciliation;
(D) incident filing;
(E) governance of protected paths;
(F) measuring held-out recall.

key_rules:
Governance: a change to a protected path needs all of the following:
- a 'User-Decision: UD-nnnn' commit trailer whose record's scope covers the change;
- a commit range that touches no delivery directory;
- a passing selftest;
- monotone strictness, meaning each incident fixture still fires a superset of its expected checks and every clean fixture still passes.
Removing an expected check, lowering a threshold floor, raising a tier threshold or changing CONSTRAINTS counts as loosening and needs the owner's verbatim decision text.

checks:
AMD-02: a row is FAIL unless it has a resolving user_decision, the old endpoint in lock.coprimary[] and a bumped protocol_id, whenever either of these holds:
- its fields_changed include any primary.* or guards field, and outcome artifacts exist before its commit;
- objective_distance increases on any axis.

Ladder, per world and pool:
- CM-CONST: the most frequent taught answer;
- CM-LENFREQ and CM-SUFFIX1..3: frequency shortcuts conditioned on prompt length or suffix;
- CM-EXACT: taught prompt-to-answer table;
- CM-CANON: canonical-key table;
- CM-COPY-A and CM-COPY-B: copy an operand;
- CM-FIXED0..8: the arm's own stream_hex truncated at k, plus EOS;
- CM-MODE: modal byte repeated to the modal length;
- chance;
- all declared control arms.

Derived quantities:
- G = mean over worlds of [exact(W, HELD, final, required strata) - max(exact(no-prior-teaching control), best shortcut, chance)];
- G_per_sample = G / taught presentations, taken from receipts;
- OLD_drop = own OLD_END - own FINAL;
- designs_tried_k = number of distinct native_closure_sha values in dev rows.

Operators, with the seed selecting which arm, item, parameter and k:
- M01: copy W's digest and writes into a lesion arm's receipt (expects ADM-CTRL);
- M02: negate a directional delta (ADM-DIR);
- M03: perturb a fixture param by 40% (ADM-FIX);
- M04: delete a gate's negative fixtures (ADM-GATE);
- M05: replace the candidate's dev outputs with its own FIXED_k stream (LAD-02 or RED);
- M06: drop a comparator's rows (LAD-01 or RED-03);
- M07: insert a held prompt into a feedback stream (ADM-EXPO);
- M08: change primary.margin_abs without an amendment (AMD-01);
- M09: insert 'largely met' beside the primary in REPORT (CLM-06);
- M10: set worlds_role SEALED_CONFIRM on dev worlds (CLM-04 or WLD-01);
- M11: add an import of a runner module to verdict.py (LOCK-08-ISO);
- M12: set a gate input to null (RED-05);
- M13: add a literal exposure_overlap = 0 (LOCK-08-LIT);
- M14: add 'confirmation_passes': true to a pointer copy (PTR-03).

tools.self_tests:
Phase-0 acceptance on real history:
- must FLAG Package B (R-CTRL), V6-R1 (R-CLOSURE, R-LITERAL), LEARNED-BANK (R-FAILOPEN, R-CLAIM 'Native confirmation'), CLS ('largely met'), and CURRENT_EXTERNAL_MEMORY (R-POINTER);
- the false-flag rate on deliveries believed clean (r_center survivor round 1, SAMPLE-GEN V2 confirm) is measured and reported before the owner freezes v1.

artifacts_and_schemas.who_checks:
minifly_check.py REV-01 requires all of the following:
- reviewer family differs from author family;
- at least one executed check with existing hashes;
- canary detected equals n;
- no open blocking finding;
- REVIEW commit precedes the first official output;
- executed-check outputs are not byte-identical to any earlier delivery's, which detects padding.

best_ideas:
U2C makes 'generalize from units' measurable, and every item below is computed by the evaluator:
- UNITS.json fixed before streams;
- strata S0-S4 computed from item content (the headline counts S2+ only);
- an evaluator-side determinacy witness never given to the learner;
- a leave-units-out unit-dependency index (UDI) across paired worlds;
- gradients per sample and per compute;
- structure meters plus a HASH_CODE mutant against distinguishability Goodhart.

implementation_work_packages.instructions:
Source of truth: the MRK-1 spec, sections 7.4 (LOCK-*, AMD-*, WIT-01), 8 (ENDPOINT_LOCK, SOURCE_LOCK, AMENDMENTS, REVIEW, AUTHORIZATION) and 10 (seal). Use only the common.py interfaces of section 7.3. Python 3.9 or later, stdlib only. Never import delivery modules: parse them with ast only.

(1) init
- cmd_init(args) writes <dir>/ENDPOINT_LOCK.json from templates/ENDPOINT_LOCK.template.json, filling in:
  - delivery name;
  - protocol_id '<dir>-p1';
  - author_agent (from --agent or the environment variable MINIFLY_AGENT, else '__FILL__');
  - constraints_sha256;
  - threshold defaults equal to CONSTRAINTS floors;
  - required arms for --claim-level;
  - testbed grids from CONSTRAINTS;
  - addition task format defaults;
  - objective_distance derived from --task-family;
  - lineage.parent from --parent;
  - prediction skeletons whose base_rate_forecast is Laplace from kb DELIVERY_LEDGER for the task family.
- If kb.deliveries_since_last_e3 (lazy import) shows the cadence is due, print that the lock must open held E3 or cite a waiver.
- Refuse to overwrite an existing lock.

(2) lock build
- cmd_lock_build computes the static AST import closure from files.native, files.runner and files.admit:
  - resolve 'import x', 'from x import y' and relative imports against the delivery dir, the repo root and literal sys.path.insert/append targets (string literals, or os.path.join/dirname compositions of literals and __file__);
  - stdlib and site-packages modules are not hashed, but their versions are recorded via importlib.metadata without importing them;
  - non-literal importlib or __import__ calls are listed and must match dynamic_imports.
- Write SOURCE_LOCK.json per section 8, including native_closure_sha, runner_closure_sha and params_sha256 of files.params.

(3) Checks
- Implement LOCK-01..13, AMD-01..05 and WIT-01 exactly as in section 7.4.
  - LOCK-06 uses ctx.git.first_commit_adding and first_commit_where.
  - LOCK-07 scans the AST of the native and runner closure with the CONSTRAINTS.native_forbidden lists. Heuristic hits return WARN at T1; at T2 they return FAIL unless REVIEW.json has a finding whose fm_ids include FM-12 or FM-23 with status other than OPEN.
  - LOCK-08 uses AST Assign, AnnAssign and dict literals.
  - LOCK-09 calls kb.deliveries_since_last_e3; if the function is missing, the result is MISSING.
  - LOCK-13 uses common.family_of.
  - AMD-02 decides 'learner-arm outcome exists' from any committed receipts or OUTPUTS rows whose arm is not CEILING_* or FLOOR_*.
  - WIT-01 checks the reviewer-namespace containment via remote_branches_containing (origin/claude/* versus origin/codex/*, opposite to lock.author_agent).

(4) seal.py
- Subcommands commit-nonce, reveal, derive and verify, per section 10:
  - the nonce comes from secrets.token_hex(32) and is written outside the repo (default ~/.minifly_nonce_<delivery>_<lock8>);
  - refuse a private path inside the repo;
  - key = sha256(nonce || owner_go_verbatim); seeds via hmac.new(key, msg, sha256);
  - reveal appends a CONFIRM_LEDGER row with attempt_index j (count of prior rows in the family) and alpha 0.05*2^-j, or as set by CONSTRAINTS.
- Implement SEAL-01..05 and CONF-01; they are NOT_APPLICABLE unless worlds.official_role_intended is SEALED_CONFIRM or CLAIM.worlds_role is SEALED_CONFIRM.

(5) Tests
- Build small TimelineGit fixtures inside the tests (temporary dirs) covering:
  - lock and results in the same commit (LOCK-06 FAIL);
  - a closure that misses an imported bootstrap.py (LOCK-05 FAIL);
  - a sys.path.insert to a sibling delivery (LOCK-05 FAIL);
  - .backward( in a native file (LOCK-07 FAIL);
  - a literal exposure_overlap = 0 (LOCK-08 FAIL);
  - a margin change without an amendment (AMD-01 FAIL);
  - a task swap after outcomes without a UD (AMD-02 FAIL);
  - a clean lock (all PASS);
  - a seal round-trip;
  - reveal before GO (SEAL-02 FAIL);
  - derived world mismatch (SEAL-04 FAIL).

Acceptance: all tests pass, and every check id above appears in common.CHECKS when lockcheck and seal are imported.

Source of truth: the MRK-1 spec, section 7.4 (ADM-*, AUT-*, REV-*, DEBT-*, CAN-*), section 8 (ADMISSION, receipts, FIXTURES, TAUGHT, AUTHORIZATION, REVIEW, REVIEW_DEBT), section 12.2 (canary operators) and section 6.4 (review protocol). Python 3.9 or later, stdlib only. Use common.py and the scorer and decide interfaces (lazy import; MissingInput if absent).

(1) admitcheck.py: implement ADM-01, ADM-TB-CEIL, ADM-TB-FLOOR, ADM-CTRL-ZERO, ADM-CTRL-ISO, ADM-CTRL-DOSE, ADM-DIR, ADM-FIX, ADM-GATE, ADM-INPUT, ADM-ACQ, ADM-SHUF, ADM-EXPO, ADM-BUDGET, ADM-REUSE and AUT-01..04.
- Read raw receipts, TAUGHT manifests and admission OUTPUTS rather than trusting ADMISSION summaries. Any disagreement between a receipt and a summary is FAIL.
- ADM-TB-* and ADM-ACQ call scorer.score_rows and decide.evaluate on the CEILING_*, FLOOR_* and prefix rows. Grid coverage must include the CONSTRAINTS grids.
- ADM-SHUF computes agreement of the SHUF arm's TAUGHT labels with the true answers (addition.expected) and orbit consistency (same canonical orbit gives the same label).
- ADM-EXPO canonicalizes held items with addition.canonical. Exact prompts apply for E2. It also lists every TAUGHT or stream file under manifests/ and results/ and fails on undeclared ones.
- ADM-CTRL-ZERO checks that write_counter_site names an existing function in the native closure (ast).
- AUT-02 extracts numbers from the verbatim text with a regex, then compares them with the caps or applies CONSTRAINTS.default_grant_caps.
- AUT-04 evaluates predicates of the form {artifact, pointer, op, value} with JSON-pointer lookup.

(2) review.py: implement REV-01..08 and DEBT-01..02.
- Identity: the Acting-Agent trailers of the commit introducing review/REVIEW.json, plus remote_branches_containing namespace matching. The family must be opposite to lock.author_agent.
- REV-02 verifies script and output files exist and that their sha256 values match.
- REV-07 compares output sha256 values against every other delivery's review/ outputs in the repo.
- REV-08 recomputes canonical row digests of the official rows for each replayed (world, arm).
- cmd_review implements 'review replay-plan <dir> --seed HEX --n 4': a deterministic choice of (world, arm) pairs from the official outputs, using an HMAC of the seed. It also implements 'review check'.

(3) canary.py: cmd_canary copies the delivery to a temporary dir, applies the role-targeted operators of section 12.2 (M01-M07, M09-M15, M17) chosen by role and behaviour with random.Random(seed), and runs only each operator's target check ids (and decide where applicable) with git checks disabled.
- It reports CAN-01..03 into <dir>/checks/CANARY_<utc>.json. That report is the only file written inside the delivery.
- An operator with no target is recorded as not applicable.

(4) Tests: synthetic receipts and rows covering, at minimum:
- Package-B-like identical digests (ADM-CTRL-ZERO FAIL);
- a confounding change on a non-declared channel (ADM-CTRL-ISO FAIL);
- dose 0.003 (ADM-CTRL-DOSE FAIL);
- a negative directional delta (ADM-DIR FAIL);
- fixture density 0.02 versus params 0.0354 (ADM-FIX FAIL);
- a derangement SHUF (ADM-SHUF FAIL);
- a commuted held item in calibration (ADM-EXPO FAIL);
- a '(Recommended)' option label (AUT-03 FAIL);
- caps absent from the verbatim text (AUT-02 FAIL);
- a same-family review (REV-01 FAIL);
- a REFUTED blocker without a countersign (REV-06 FAIL);
- a copied output (REV-07 FAIL);
- canary on a clean synthetic delivery with all applicable mutants caught.

Acceptance: all tests pass, and the registered ids match RULES.json.
